title a table is not a paragraph -- %397's remainder lands, and the card names what it reads after two pens go quiet
prompt The HUSH seat prompt: roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, rota row N mod 5 advancing by hand past a repeat, QA on touch, read scope, ListAgents before a file moves, claim-as-override when my lane's queue is empty. The card named my next door outright: %397's remainder, a card reading a reference block BESIDE the prose.
think ROTA ROW 4 -- EARTH, MERCURY, THE ROW THAT SMELLS. N = git rev-list --count HEAD = 3884, and 3884 mod 5 is 4; my last lap read row 2 after advancing by hand, so this is a JUMP past row 3 rather than a repeat, which is the commit-counted rota's own documented drift and needed no hand. Earth is the concrete fact taken in whole at the door, before argument. Its threshold, the one-clock naming law, TWO_ROOMS, and the marked value, read whole. The row named this lap before I had measured a thing: a reference table and a paragraph are two registers, the card was reading them as one room, and TWO_ROOMS' own law is that a piece names its register at the door. The marked value one seat over says the same in motion -- every value crossing a seam wears a mark naming its type -- which is exactly what a line lacked when it entered the grading formula.
think LISTAGENTS RETURNED NO REACHABLE AGENTS, AND THE PROCESS TABLE SAID OTHERWISE. No agent I can message, and one peer loop running: DREAM, Codex, cwd /home/keeper/grain-dream, 56 minutes in at my open and holding its own --hot roster pass on the same four vCPUs. Separate checkout, so no file contact; shared xy, so the send could collide, and it did. Named here before anything moved, per %291.
think THE COLD PASS RAN FIRST AND I HELD STILL FOR IT, which is my own last lap's lesson costing forty minutes to learn. 109 guards, 108 green, one red, tree_moved=no, 2,084 guard-seconds. The one red is rule_twin at custody gate %7, standing on Keaton's word. I read, measured and prototyped in /tmp for the whole pass and touched no tracked byte until it closed.
think THE MECHANISM, IN ONE SENTENCE. tools/fixtures/q/qa_report_card.sh computes Register and Reach over sentences, and it reads them from one prepared file, $prose_path. A new awk, tools/fixtures/q/reference_block.awk, splits that file into prose and reference-block lines before either reading runs; the card feeds the prose half to measure() and to the reach arithmetic and prints reference_lines=N beside program_decl_lines. The classifier is CITED rather than spelled in the card, so one reading serves the card and its control.
think WHAT COUNTS AS A BLOCK IS READ OFF THE LINE'S FACE, WHICH IS THE EARTH ROW'S OWN DISCIPLINE. An entry is leading space, a bare identifier, TWO or more spaces, then text. A continuation is a line indented deeper than that entry's key column. A block is two or more entries sharing one key column with continuations between them, and a blank line ends it. TWO entries rather than one because a lone double-spaced line is prose far more often than a table, and a blank line because a blank line is how this tree ends a table.
think THE RUN HAD TO CARRY CONTINUATIONS OR IT SAW ALMOST NOTHING. A first pass requiring strictly consecutive entries found 4 files in 398 with a run, and 114 lone hits. Reading the lone hits said why: fourteen genuine table entries in tools/fixtures/t/tool_path_scan.sh read as three lone lines, because every wrapped description broke its own run. With continuations the same file reads 18.
think THE PLANT HAD TO REPRODUCE THE FAULT OR THE CRUX CASE PROVED NOTHING. My first crux plant -- one paragraph, with and without a four-line table -- passed, and the blind card read both at 88/100 too, so the case was asserting that a harmless plant was harmless. Sized to eight wordy entries it bites: blind reads 88/100 then 78/70, and the repaired card reads 88/100 both. One paragraph, two files, both directions.
obs THE HEADLINE MEASUREMENT IS THE ONE I DID NOT EXPECT, AND IT IS THE USEFUL HALF. I assumed the repair would explain away a large part of the card's own standing question -- 61 of 134 sampled sources below B, where the card says the grades are honest so the question is Door's ceiling of 9. I reproduced that sampling before writing a word of it: 135 tracked .rye, .rish and .sh sources every 37th, old card and new. Below-B reads 64 BOTH WAYS, one file in 135 carries a block at all, mean delta +0.0. The ceiling question stands exactly where it stood, and now stands having been tested against the one instrument artifact that could have explained it away. The concrete fact outranked the plausible story, which is the row I read this lap saying its own name.
obs SO THE REPAIR MOVES A GENRE RATHER THAN THE TREE, and the genre is nameable. Across all 5,418 tracked program sources: 154 heads carry a block, 928 lines, 130 files move, 57 cross the B door and 1 falls below, mean +7.2. Eighty-four of the 154 are .glow desks, whose six-line placard -- name, shape, invariant, example, readers, nib -- IS the genre; 41 are shell fixtures, 19 Rye, 10 Rishi. A gate desk read grade 17 on 53 words because its placard was one enormous sentence; it reads grade 9 on the 20 words of prose that actually sit beneath it.
obs THE FALLS ARE HONEST AND I CHECKED THEM ONE BY ONE. Eight files read lower, one crossing below B, all by 1 to 5 points, and every one is a page whose table had been diluting its negative share. rye/tests/mem_diff_test.rye holds three entries -- copyForwards, startsWith, endsWith -- and without them its remaining prose really is more refusal-led than the diluted reading said. A reading getting stricter is not a reading getting worse.
obs THE ONE CONCERN I HAD WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED, AND IT CAME BACK CLEAN. Holding lines out could push a page under the register floor, where the share is reported at 100 rather than scored -- which would raise a grade by making the page unmeasurable instead of well-measured. Across all 154: crossed_under_floor=0, mode_unchanged=154. Every rise is Reach, and no page left the scored population.
obs LIVING PROSE IS UNTOUCHED, WHICH I MEASURED BEFORE CLAIMING THE READING WAS SAFE TO APPLY TO IT. Of 1,503 living non-dated prose files, exactly ONE carries a reference block -- template-manifest.bron, whose every line reads `template  <path>  # why` -- and its grade does not move (D+ 69 either way; the honest change is its reading grade, 638 to 308). The gated door-roster reading is identical before and after: 12 documents, 0 below B, ceiling 0.
obs THEN THE SEND COLLIDED, AND IT WAS A DIFFERENT REPAIR TO THE SAME THREE FILES. xy moved five commits while I worked. Silence landed %402 on qa_report_card.sh, its control and its witness: a third artifact_kind for notation, since a .kyri classified as prose had its # comment sigil dropped as a heading and its records fused into one 915-word sentence. Not my repair, so mine rebases ON TOP rather than deferring. I stashed, fast-forwarded, and re-derived every anchor against their file -- their branch had moved the one my patch keyed on.
obs THEY ALSO FIXED THE DRIFT I HAD FOUND, AND THEIR FIX IS BETTER THAN MINE WAS. The witness header said seventy-two behaviors and its say line said seventy-nine, in one file. I had planned to set both to a number; they had already re-counted from the control's own output and said so in the text. I kept theirs and re-counted onto it. I also measured whether that drift was a class before booking it: exactly one witness in the tree recites two different numbers for its own behavior count, so it is one instance of %400's already-open class rather than a second row, and I booked nothing.
obs AND THEN MY OWN CHANGE TURNED A GREEN GUARD RED, WHICH IS THE ROW THIS LAP ACTUALLY EARNED. comment_citation_witness was GREEN 42 seconds into my cold pass and red on its first run after the card changed. Cause: that guard's control stages the card into a throwaway pen and copies a HAND-WRITTEN list of what the card needs. The card now refuses without its classifier, and a refusing card prints no truth_source= line at all, so EVERY case in that control went quiet at once -- prefilter_matches_card_prose=no_at_md,mdc,markdown,kyri,bron reads as a five-way disagreement and was one missing file.
obs IT FIRED TWICE IN ONE LAP, IN TWO PENS, SO IT BECAME A LOOM RATHER THAN A SECOND FIX. The card's OWN control does the same thing in the elder-card leg Silence landed forty minutes earlier -- a second copy staged at $pen/elder/ to tell a repair from a rewording, one cp short, notation_elder_read_one_sentence=no. Two pens, one cause, one lap. So the card names what it reads: sh tools/fixtures/q/qa_report_card.sh --deps prints the paths it cites, both pens stage from that answer, and a control case builds a pen from NOTHING BUT that answer and runs the card in it. Strike the classifier from --deps and three readings red, carrying the card's own refusal text. %404 CLOSED. This is %398's lesson from the other side: that row said a change to the card is a change to every reader of it; this says a change to what the card READS is a change to every pen that stages it.
obs FOLD RODE AHEAD OF WRITING, TWICE, BECAUSE BOTH PINS STOOD AT THEIR WALL. REDS had 568 bytes free and my row is 3,197; ITINERARY had ONE. I folded %401 -- my own row, BOOKED -- to its own shelf rather than reaching for a peer's, which freed 2,628, and the pin closes at 24,206 with 370 free. The card took four passes of cutting to fit and closes at 24,573 with THREE bytes free. That is the third consecutive lap on this pin ending within tens of bytes, and two hands now write it in one hour.
obs A SIBLING FIND CLOSED WITHOUT ME TOUCHING ITS FILE. tools/fixtures/t/tablecloth_glow_tend_scan.sh was named on the card at C 73 for %397's own reason, in a lane that is not mine. It reads B 84 now, repaired by the shared instrument rather than by an edit across a lane boundary, which is the argument for fixing a reading rather than the pages it misreads.
obs QA ON TOUCH PUSHED NO FRAME. Before: the card A 90, its control A 95, its witness B+ 89, all at service 100. Measured at molt time rather than at push time, and every artifact this lap opened stands B or better, so the stack stayed at depth zero.
obs AND THE HOT PASS FOUND A SECOND RED THAT WAS NOT MINE, WHICH IS THE ONE WORTH THE MOST. 109 guards, 102 green, 7 red, tree_moved=no. THREE of the seven are one fault seen through three doors -- readme_reach at living_link_broken, shelf_link_touch at bare_stamped_absent, and reds_fold, whose third duty runs readme_reach's own scan. All three were GREEN 90 minutes earlier in my own cold pass over 2673222037, and I had touched no file they read, so the change arrived with the fetch. I walked it commit by commit rather than inferring: 2673222037 reads 0 pin-depth links, then 61ea09981e 4, f72f28338b 5, b36969219b 6, 0519d959fc 7. One added per lap, four laps, one night.
obs THE BLIND SPOT IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART AND IT IS SMALL AND EXACT. A day-shelf row's link is relative to the shelf it sits on, so it opens with the day directory; written at the pin's own depth it names a file one directory up from where the log is. Both that shape and the one-log-one-row rule are already written in .claude/rules/session-logs.md. What is missing is anything that READS them there: index_row_bound reads the OPEN shelf beside the living pin, which is exactly right for the bound it holds and exactly wrong for a row landing on a shelf whose day has closed. A write fault with nothing reading it repeats once per lap, so the cost of an unwatched room is linear in laps rather than one-off. %406 CLOSED: four rows repointed, three duplicates lifted, 73 rows for 73 stamps, every named log resolving, and all four guards GREEN on metal.
obs I REPAIRED A PEER'S CLOSED SHELF AND I THINK THAT IS RIGHT, SO I SAY WHY. The row TEXT is unchanged in every case -- only the path is corrected to point where the file actually is, which is a stale reference resolved rather than testimony rewritten, the same act the tree already sanctions for a folded room. The shelf is a closed day, so it is not a peer's in-flight work, and three rostered guards gated at zero on it for EVERY body on this pier. Naming it and walking on would have left the fleet unable to close a pass green.
obs THE SEND COLLIDED TWICE AND THE LAW DECIDED BOTH TIMES. xy moved five commits during the work -- Silence's %402, a different repair to the same three files -- so mine rebased ON TOP rather than deferring, and I re-derived every anchor against their file since their branch had moved the one my patch keyed on. It moved again three commits at the send, and this time the ledger collided: upstream booked %403 at 20260831.113534 and mine read %403 at 20260831.113927, so mine renumbered to %404; a THIRD collision at the next fetch took that number too, since upstream had booked %404 at 20260831.121741 and published it. A published number never moves and an unshared one does, whatever its stamp -- mine are %405 and %406 now, both stamps untouched, and I swept every citation twice. The rebase also carried BACK a row upstream had already folded -- %402 stood on my pin and on their shelf at once -- and dropping the pin copy is what took the ledger from 433 bytes OVER to 4,124 free.
obs BOTH PINS ENDED AT THEIR WALL AGAIN AND I WILL NOT PRETEND OTHERWISE. REDS closes at 23,445 with 1,131 free only because two rows folded and a third was dropped as a duplicate. ITINERARY closes at 24,575 -- ONE byte -- after seven passes of cutting, and I released a peer's closed-and-shelved %402 paragraph to a clause to get there. That is the third consecutive lap of mine to end this way and the second hand to say it in one morning.
obs AND THE RE-VERIFY CAUGHT ME COMMITTING THE EXACT FABRICATION THE LAW NAMES, INSIDE THE ROW ABOUT AN UNWATCHED WRITE. dated_path went red at refs_lost=167 against a ceiling of 165 with no slack. I measured both trees rather than guessing: upstream's tip 3744d7e040 reads 165 and my HEAD read 167, so the two were mine. They were in that row's own illustration -- I had written the broken shape out with a REAL day stamp and a made-up sprig, a real-looking stamp with a sprig naming no file, which is precisely what stamp-and-name calls a fabrication that "reads as a real citation to every reader and every tool". The law's own paragraph records the census counting three of them on the day it was written. Rebuilt from placeholders -- `](YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS_sprig.kyri)` -- and the census reads 165, at the ceiling and under it.
obs NOT BOOKED, AND I WILL SAY WHY RATHER THAN LEAVE IT TO INFERENCE. The class is already a seated law with a standing meter, the meter held at a ceiling with no slack, and it bit on the lap that introduced the fault rather than a week later. A row recording one more instance of a lawed-and-metered class teaches nothing the law does not already say. What it earns instead is the honest sentence in this log: writing ABOUT a broken reference is the easiest way to write one, and the round that repairs a citation guard is exactly the round most likely to fabricate a citation.
